  "resultText": "When the clinic cancels an appointment with `{ \"reason\": \"clinic\", \"refund\": true }`, the no-show invoice is now marked `REFUNDED`. `npm test` passes (34 unit and 7 e2e tests), and typecheck and lint are clean.\n\n- **Cancelling after a no-show:** `Appointment.cancel` used to accept only `SCHEDULED` appointments. It now also lets a `NO_SHOW` appointment be cancelled when the reason is exactly `clinic`. Any other reason still returns 422, so patients can't cancel their way out of the fee.\n- **Event:** `AppointmentCancelled` has a new `refund: boolean` field. It is true only if the request asked for a refund and the appointment was actually cancelled by the clinic. A `refund: true` with another reason (say `sick`) is ignored.\n- **Billing:** the billing listener now handles that event. When `refund` is true it refunds the no-show invoice for that appointment.\n  - No invoice, or an already-refunded one: nothing happens.\n  - Billing never imports scheduling code; it only sees the event.\n- **Invoice:** it has a new `REFUNDED` status and a `refundedAt` timestamp. `GET /billing/invoices` now returns `refundedAt`, which is `null` for open invoices.\n- **Schema change:** `BillingInvoice.refundedAt` is a new nullable column. I ran `prisma generate`. Any existing database needs `npm run db:push` (the e2e setup does this itself).\n- **Repository:** `save` is now an upsert instead of a create, so refunds update the existing row.\n\nI added tests at domain, service and e2e level. The e2e cases cover a clinic cancel with refund (invoice becomes `REFUNDED`), a clinic cancel without refund (invoice stays `OPEN`), and a non-clinic cancel of a no-show (422).\n\nIf a refund fails inside billing, the appointment is already saved as cancelled. This matches how the existing event flow works, and I didn't add a compensating step.",
